 ],
 "literal": "Every place is our hometown, everyone is our kinsman. / Evil and good do not come given by others. / Pain and its easing are like them. / Dying is not a new thing; living / we do not rejoice saying 'it is sweet', nor in dislike / do we say 'it is bitter'. With lightning / the sky pours cool drops, and without ceasing / battering rocks, roars the fertile big river; / like a raft that goes along the path of water, precious life / goes along the path of destiny, this, the wise / have realized through their vision; therefore, in greatness / we do not marvel at the great, / and despising the small is even less possible for us.",
 "translation": "Every town is our hometown, and all people are our kinsmen. Good and evil do not come from others, nor do pain and its relief. Death is nothing new, nor do we rejoice in life as sweet, or despise it as bitter in anger. Just as a raft drifts along the path of a mighty river, which roars as it batters rocks, fed by cool rain pouring from lightning-filled clouds, so does our precious life drift along the path of destiny. Since we have realized this through the vision of wise sages, we do not marvel at the great, and much less do we look down upon the small.",
 "modern": "எல்லா ஊர்களும் நம் ஊரே; எல்லா மக்களும் நம் உறவினரே. நன்மையும் தீமையும் பிறரால் நமக்கு வருவதில்லை. துன்பமும் அதன் தணிவும் அவற்றைப் போன்றவையே. இறப்பது புதியது அன்று; வாழ்வது இனிது என்று நாம் மகிழ்வதும் இல்லை; வெறுப்பினால் அது இன்னாதது என்று சலிப்பதும் இல்லை. மின்னலுடன் மேகம் குளிர்ந்த துளிகளைப் பொழிய, ஓயாமல் கல்லை முட்டி ஒலிக்கும் வளமான பெரிய ஆற்றின் நீர்வழியே செல்லும் மிதவையைப் போல, அரிய உயிர் ஊழ்வழியே செல்லும் என்பதை அறிஞர்களின் அறிவுக் காட்சியால் தெளிந்தோம். ஆதலால், பெருமையுடைய பெரியோரை வியந்து பாராட்டுவதும் இல்லை; சிறியோரை இகழ்ந்து பேசுவது அதனினும் இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "This famous poem is spoken by the poet Kaniyan Pungundranar under the theme of Poruṇmoḻik Kāñci (words of wisdom) in the Potuviyal tiṇai (general themes). The poet presents a profound, universal philosophy of life, declaring that all places are our home and all people are our relatives. He explains that happiness and suffering, life and death, are natural occurrences and not caused by others. Using the vivid image of a raft drifting down a roaring mountain river fed by monsoon rains, he illustrates how human life is carried along by the inevitable flow of destiny. Having realized this truth through the wisdom of sages, the poet concludes that we should neither be awed by the great nor look down upon the humble, maintaining an equanimous mind.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}